{
  "dom_id": "step-q103701",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "If $sec\u2061x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2},$ then what is $sec\u2061(x+14\\pi )?$",
  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061x=sec\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec\u2061x=sec\u2061(x+2\\cdot 7\\cdot \\pi )=sec\u2061(x+14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec\u2061x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2},$ then $sec\u2061(x+14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}.$",
